The '''Great Wall of China'''<ref>({{zh|t=萬里長城|s=万里长城|p=Wànlǐ Chángchéng|lit="Ten-Thousand Mile Long Wall"|first=t}}, literally "ten thousand [[Li (unit)|''li'']] long wall")</ref> is a series of [[fortifications]] in China. They were built across the historical northern borders of [[ancient Chinese states]] and [[Imperial China]] as protection against [[Eurasian nomads|various nomadic groups]] from the [[Eurasian Steppe]]. The first walls date to the 7th century BC; these were joined together in the [[Qin dynasty]].<ref name="nyt">{{cite book |author=''[[The New York Times]]'' with introduction by [[Sam Tanenhaus]] |title=The New York Times Guide to Essential Knowledge: A Desk Reference for the Curious Mind |publisher=[[St. Martin's Press]] of [[Macmillan Publishers]] |year=2011 |isbn=978-0-312-64302-7 |page=1131 |quote=Beginning as separate sections of fortification around the 7th century B.C.E and unified during the Qin Dynasty in the 3rd century B.C.E, this wall, built of earth and rubble with a facing of brick or stone, runs from east to west across China for over 4,000 miles. }}</ref><ref name=Brit>{{cite encyclopedia |url=https://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/243863/Great-Wall-of-China |title=Great Wall of China |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |date=October 21, 2023 |quote=Large parts of the fortification system date from the 7th through the 4th century BC. In the 3rd century BC Shihuangdi (Qin Shi Huang), the first emperor of a united China (under the Qin dynasty), connected a number of existing defensive walls into a single system. Traditionally, the eastern terminus of the wall was considered to be [[Shanhai Pass]] (Shanhaiguan) on the coast of the Bohai (Gulf of Zhili), and the wall's length{{snd}}without its branches and other secondary sections{{snd}}was thought to extend for some {{cvt|4160|mi|km |order=flip }}. |access-date=June 21, 2022 |archive-date=May 2, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150502181216/http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/243863/Great-Wall-of-China |url-status=live }}</ref> Successive dynasties expanded the wall system; the best-known sections were built by the [[Ming dynasty]] (1368–1644).

To aid in defense, the Great Wall utilized [[watchtower]]s, troop barracks, garrison stations, signaling capabilities through the means of smoke or fire, and its status as a transportation corridor. Other purposes of the Great Wall have included [[border control]]s (allowing control of immigration and emigration, and the imposition of duties on goods transported along the [[Silk Road]]), and the regulation of trade.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Shelach-Lavi |first1=Gideon |author1-link=Gideon Shelach-Lavi |last2=Wachtel |first2=Ido |last3=Golan |first3=Dan |last4=Batzorig |first4=Otgonjargal |last5=Amartuvshin |first5=Chunag |last6=Ellenblum |first6=Ronnie |last7=Honeychurch |first7=William |date=June 2020 |title=Medieval long-wall construction on the Mongolian Steppe during the eleventh to thirteenth centuries AD |journal=Antiquity |language=en |volume=94 |issue=375 |pages=724–741 |doi=10.15184/aqy.2020.51 |issn=0003-598X |doi-access=free }}</ref> 

The collective fortifications constituting the Great Wall stretch from [[Liaodong Peninsula|Liaodong]] in the east to [[Lop Nur|Lop Lake]] in the west, and from the present-day [[China–Russia border|Sino-Russian border]] in the north to [[Tao River]] in the south: an arc that roughly delineates the edge of the [[Mongolian steppe]], spanning {{cvt|21196.18|km|mi}} in total.<ref name="Great Wall of China CBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/great-wall-of-china-even-longer-than-previously-thought-1.1263111 |title=Great Wall of China even longer than previously thought |publisher=[[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation]] |date=June 6, 2012 |access-date=June 6, 2012 |archive-date=December 14, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131214152338/https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/great-wall-of-china-even-longer-than-previously-thought-1.1263111 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="ncha"/> It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and was voted one of the [[New 7 Wonders of the World]] in 2007.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Centre |first=UNESCO World Heritage |title=The Great Wall |url=https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/438 |access-date=January 6, 2025 |website=UNESCO World Heritage Centre |language=en |archive-date=July 31, 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080731174854/https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/438 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=August 8, 2016 |title=Great Wall of China |url=https://world.new7wonders.com/wonders/great-wall-of-china-220-b-c-and-1368-1644-a-d-china/ |access-date=January 6, 2025 |website=New7Wonders of the World |language=en-US |archive-date=June 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624034714/https://world.new7wonders.com/wonders/great-wall-of-china-220-b-c-and-1368-1644-a-d-china/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Today, the defensive system of the Great Wall is recognized as one of the most impressive architectural feats in history.<ref name="Great Wall of China">{{cite news |url=http://www.history.com/topics/great-wall-of-china |title=Great Wall of China |publisher=History |date=April 20, 2009 |archive-date=February 8, 2018 |access-date=September 16, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180208000939/http://www.history.com/topics/great-wall-of-china |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Names==
[[File:華夷圖附注.jpg|thumb|left|''[[Huayi tu]]'', an 1136 map of China with the Great Wall depicted on the northern edge of the country]]
The collection of fortifications known as the Great Wall of China has historically had a number of different names in both Chinese and English.

In [[Chinese historiography|Chinese histories]], the term "Long Wall(s)" ({{nowrap|<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> 長城,}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> 长城,}} ''Chángchéng'') appears in [[Sima Qian]]'s ''[[Records of the Grand Historian]]'', where it referred both to the separate great walls built between and north of the [[Warring States]] and to the more unified construction of the [[First Emperor]].{{sfn|Waldron|1983|p=650}} The [[Chinese character]] {{lang|zh|{{linktext|城}}}}, meaning city or fortress, is a [[phono-semantic compound]] of the [[earth radical|"earth" radical]] {{lang|zh|{{linktext|土}}}} and phonetic {{lang|zh|{{linktext|成}}}}, whose [[Old Chinese]] pronunciation has been [[linguistic reconstruction|reconstructed]] as *''deŋ''.<ref>{{cite web |last=Baxter |first=William H. &&nbsp;al. |url=http://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf |title=Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese Reconstruction, Version 1.1 |publisher=University of Michigan |location=Ann Arbor |date=September 20, 2014 |access-date=January 22, 2015 |archive-date=October 10, 2022 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20221010/http://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref> It originally referred to the [[Chinese city wall|rampart]] which surrounded [[ancient Chinese urban planning|traditional Chinese cities]] and was used by extension for these walls around [[ancient Chinese states|their respective states]]; today, however, it is more commonly used to mean "city".<ref>See Lovell 2006, p. 25</ref>

The longer Chinese name "Ten-Thousand Mile Long Wall" ({{nowrap|<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> 萬里長城,}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> 万里长城,}} ''Wànlǐ Chángchéng'') came from Sima Qian's description of it in the ''Records'', though he did not name the walls themselves as such. The AD 493 ''[[Book of Song]]'' quotes the frontier general [[Tan Daoji]] referring to "the long wall of 10,000 miles", closer to the modern name, but the name rarely features in pre-modern times otherwise.{{sfn|Waldron|1990|p=202|ps=. Tan Daoji's exact quote: "So you would destroy your Great Wall of Ten Thousand ''Li''!" (乃復壞汝萬里之長城) Note the use of the [[Chinese particles|particle]] [[wikt:之|之]] ''zhi'' that differentiates the quote from the modern name.}} The [[traditional Chinese mile]] ({{lang|zh|{{linktext|里}}}}, ''lǐ'') was an often irregular distance that was intended to show the length of a standard village and varied with terrain but was usually [[traditional Chinese units|standardized]] at distances around a third of an [[English mile]] (540 m).<ref name="Winborn1994">{{cite book |author=Byron R. Winborn |title=Wen Bon: a Naval Air Intelligence Officer behind Japanese lines in China |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TD-x0Heq0uEC&pg=PA63 |year=1994 |publisher=University of North Texas Press |isbn=978-0-929398-77-8 |page=63 }}</ref> However, this use of "ten-thousand" (''wàn'') is [[Literal and figurative language|figurative]] in a similar manner to the Greek and English ''[[myriad]]'' and simply means "innumerable" or "immeasurable".<ref name=lindy>{{cite book |last=Lindesay |first=William |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Y9p2JgBPMBwC&pg=PA21 |title=The Great Wall Revisited: From the Jade Gate to Old Dragon's Head |page=21 |publisher=Wuzhou Publishing |location=Beijing |date=2007 |isbn=978-7-5085-1032-3 }}</ref>

Because of the wall's association with the First Emperor's [[Qin Shihuang#Historiography|supposed tyranny]], the [[list of Chinese dynasties|Chinese dynasties]] after [[Qin dynasty|Qin]] usually avoided referring to their own additions to the wall by the name "Long Wall".{{sfn|Waldron|1983|p=651}} Instead, various terms were used in medieval records, including "frontier(s)" ({{lang|zh|{{linktext|塞}}}}, ''Sài''),{{sfn|Lovell|2006|p=15}} "rampart(s)" ({{lang|zh|{{linktext|垣}}}}, ''Yuán''),{{sfn|Lovell|2006|p=15}} "barrier(s)" ({{lang|zh|{{linktext|障}}}}, ''Zhàng''),{{sfn|Lovell|2006|p=15}} "the outer fortresses" {{nowrap|({{lang|zh|{{linktext|外|堡}}}},}} ''Wàibǎo''),{{sfn|Waldron|1990|p=49}} and "the border wall(s)" {{nowrap|(<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|邊|牆}}}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|边|墙}}}},}} ''Biānqiáng'').{{sfn|Waldron|1983|p=651}} Poetic and informal names for the wall included "the Purple Frontier" {{nowrap|({{lang|zh|{{linktext|紫|塞}}}},}} ''Zǐsài''){{sfn|Waldron|1990|p=21}} and "the Earth Dragon" {{nowrap|(<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|土|龍}}}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|土|龙}}}},}} ''Tǔlóng'').{{sfn|Waldron|1988|p=69}} Only during the [[Qing dynasty|Qing period]] did "Long Wall" become the catch-all term to refer to the many border walls regardless of their location or dynastic origin, equivalent to the English "Great Wall".{{sfn|Hessler|2007|p=59}}

The current English name evolved from accounts of {{nowrap|"the Chinese wall"}} from early modern European travelers.{{sfn|Hessler|2007|p=59}} By the nineteenth century,{{sfn|Hessler|2007|p=59}} "the Great Wall of China" had become standard in English and French, although other European languages such as German continue to refer to it as "the Chinese wall".<ref name="lindy" /> 

Sections of the wall in south [[Gobi Desert]] and [[Mongolian steppe]] are sometimes referred to as "Wall of Genghis Khan", even though [[Genghis Khan]] did not construct any walls or permanent defense lines himself.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Man |first1=John |url=http://www.adlibris.com/se/bok/great-wall-9780553817683 |title=The Great Wall: The extraordinary history of China's wonder of the world |date=2008 |publisher=TRANSWORLD PUBLISHERS LTD |isbn=9780553817683 |pages=132–148 |language=English |chapter=6. WALL-HUNT IN THE GOBI |archive-date=November 12, 2016 |access-date=February 6, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161112214348/http://www.adlibris.com/se/bok/great-wall-9780553817683 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

