==History==
{{Main|History of the Great Wall of China}}

===Early walls===
[[File:GreatWallofQinDynasty.png|thumb|left|The Great Wall of the [[Qin dynasty|Qin]] stretches from [[Lintao]] to Liaodong.]]
{{Further|Great Wall of Qi}}
The Chinese were already familiar with the techniques of [[wall|wall-building]] by the time of the [[Spring and Autumn period]] between the 8th and 5th centuries BC.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.chiculture.net/1203/html/1203b04_01.html |script-title=zh:歷代王朝修長城 |publisher=Chiculture.net |access-date=October 24, 2010 |language=zh |archive-date=June 26, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130626130821/http://www.chiculture.net/1203/html/1203b04_01.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> During this time and the subsequent [[Warring States period]], the states of [[Zheng (state)|Zheng]], [[Chu (state)|Chu]], [[Qin (state)|Qin]], [[Wei (state)|Wei]], [[Zhao (state)|Zhao]], [[Qi (state)|Qi]], [[Lu (state)|Lu]], [[Han (Warring States)|Han]], [[Yan (state)|Yan]], [[Zhongshan (state)|Zhongshan]] and [[Zhou dynasty|Zhou]] all constructed extensive fortifications to defend their own borders.<ref>{{cite web |script-title=zh:古代长城 – 战争与和平的纽带 |url=http://www.newsmth.net/bbsanc.php?path=%2Fgroups%2Fsci.faq%2FGeography%2F11%2FGeneral%2FM.1186373252.W0 |access-date=October 24, 2010 |publisher=Newsmth.net |language=zh |archive-date=November 25, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191125023728/http://www.newsmth.net/bbsanc.php?path=%2Fgroups%2Fsci.faq%2FGeography%2F11%2FGeneral%2FM.1186373252.W0 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |script-title=zh:万里长城 |url=http://www.newsmth.net/bbsanc.php?path=%2Fgroups%2Fsci.faq%2FGeography%2F11%2FGeneral%2FM.1186373660.W0 |access-date=October 24, 2010 |publisher=Newsmth.net |language=zh |archive-date=November 25, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191125023734/http://www.newsmth.net/bbsanc.php?path=%2Fgroups%2Fsci.faq%2FGeography%2F11%2FGeneral%2FM.1186373660.W0 |url-status=live }}</ref> Built to withstand the attack of small arms such as swords and spears, these walls were made mostly of stone or by stamping earth and gravel between board frames.

[[File:GreatWallOfHanDynasty.png|thumb|The Great Wall of the [[Han dynasty|Han]] is the longest of all walls, from Mamitu, near [[Yumenguan]], to Liaodong.]]

The state of [[Qin (state)|Qin]] emerged victorious in 221 BC; its ruler, now the [[First Emperor]] of a unified China, intended to centralize rule and prevent the resurgence of feudal lords; in doing so, he ordered the destruction of the sections of the walls that divided his empire among the former states. To position the empire against the [[Xiongnu]] people from the north, however, he ordered the building of new walls to connect the remaining fortifications along the northern frontier. "Build and move on" was a central guiding principle in constructing the wall, implying that the Chinese were not erecting a permanently fixed border.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Empires in World History: Power and the Politics of Difference |last1=Burbank |first1=Jane |last2=Cooper |first2=Frederick |publisher=Princeton University Press |year=2010 |location=Princeton, New Jersey |pages=45 }}</ref> 

Transporting the large quantity of materials required for construction was difficult, so builders always tried to use local resources; stone was used in montane areas, while [[rammed earth]] was used while building in the plains. There are no surviving historical records indicating the exact length and course of the Qin walls, as most of the ancient walls have eroded away over the centuries, and very few sections remain today. Later, the [[Han dynasty|Han]], the [[Northern dynasties]] and the [[Sui dynasty|Sui]] all repaired, rebuilt, or expanded sections of the Great Wall at great cost to defend themselves against northern invaders.<ref name="mongolia">{{Cite news |last=Coonan |first=Clifford |date=February 27, 2012 |title=British researcher discovers piece of Great Wall 'marooned outside China' |url=http://www.irishtimes.com/newspaper/world/2012/0227/1224312436539.html |access-date=February 28, 2012 |newspaper=[[The Irish Times]] |archive-date=February 28, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120228113806/http://www.irishtimes.com/newspaper/world/2012/0227/1224312436539.html |url-status=live }}</ref>{{sfn|Waldron|1983|p=653}} The [[Tang dynasty|Tang]] and [[Song dynasty|Song]] dynasties did not undertake any significant effort in the region.{{sfn|Waldron|1983|p=653}} Dynasties founded by non-Han ethnic groups also built border walls: the [[Xianbei]]-ruled [[Northern Wei]], the [[Khitan people|Khitan]]-ruled [[Liao dynasty|Liao]], [[Jurchen people|Jurchen]]-led [[Jin dynasty (1115–1234)|Jin]] and the [[Tangut people|Tangut]]-established [[Western Xia]], who ruled vast territories over Northern China throughout centuries, all constructed defensive walls, albeit being further north—reaching into the environs of present-day [[Mongolia]]—than Han-built fortifications.{{sfnm|Waldron|1983|1p=654|Haw|2006|2pp=52–54}}

===Ming and Qing eras===
{{Main|Ming Great Wall}}
The [[Ming dynasty]] made substantial contributions to the Great Wall, following their defeat to the [[Oirats]] in the [[Battle of Tumu]]. This defeat had come in the context of protracted conflict with [[Mongols|Mongol]] tribes; a new strategy for defense was thus realized by constructing walls along the northern border of China. Acknowledging the Mongol control established in the [[Ordos Desert]], the wall followed the desert's southern edge, instead of incorporating the bend of the [[Yellow River]].

Unlike the earlier fortifications, the Ming construction was stronger and more elaborate, due to the use of bricks and stone instead of rammed earth. Up to 25,000 watchtowers are estimated to have been constructed on the wall.{{sfn|Szabó|Dávid|Loczy|2010|p=220}} As [[Mongol invasions|Mongol raids]] continued periodically over the years, the Ming devoted considerable resources to repair and reinforce the walls; sections near the Ming capital of Beijing were especially strong.{{sfn|Evans|2006|p=177}} Under general [[Qi Jiguang]]'s supervision, 1,200 watchtowers from Shanhaiguan Pass to Changping were constructed between 1567 and 1570, and sections of the ram-earth wall were faced with bricks.<ref>{{cite web |title=Great Wall at Mutianyu |url=http://great-wall.org/mutianyu.htm |publisher=Great Wall of China |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130309091217/http://great-wall.org/mutianyu.htm |archive-date=March 9, 2013 }}</ref> 

During the mid–15th century, the Ming also built a so-called "Liaodong Wall". It enclosed the agricultural heartland of the [[Liaodong]] province, protecting it against potential incursions by Jurchen-Mongol Oriyanghan from the northwest and the [[Jianzhou Jurchens]] from the north. While stones and tiles were sometimes used here, it was otherwise simply an earth dike with moats on both sides.{{sfn|Edmonds|1985|pp=38–40}}

Towards the end of the Ming, the Great Wall helped defend the empire against the [[Manchu]] invasions that began around 1600. Even after the loss of all of [[Liaoning|Liaodong]], the Ming army held the heavily fortified [[Shanhai Pass]], preventing the Manchus from conquering the Chinese heartland. The Manchus were finally able to cross the Great Wall in 1644, after Beijing had already fallen to [[Li Zicheng]]'s short-lived [[Shun dynasty]]. Before this time, the Manchus had crossed the Great Wall multiple times to raid, but this time it was for conquest. The gates at Shanhai Pass were opened on May 25 by the commanding Ming general, [[Wu Sangui]], who formed an alliance with the Manchus, hoping to use the Manchus to expel the rebels from Beijing.{{sfn|Lovell|2006|p=254}} The Manchus quickly seized Beijing instead, and eventually defeated both the Shun dynasty and the [[Southern Ming|remaining Ming resistance]], consolidating the rule of the [[Qing dynasty]] over all of [[China proper]].{{sfn|Elliott|2001|pp=1–2}}

Under Qing rule and the [[Dzungar–Qing Wars|annexation of Mongolia into the empire]], China's borders extended beyond the Great Wall; work on it for the purpose of border defense was thus discontinued. Construction nevertheless persisted with projects like the [[Willow Palisade]]; following a line similar to that of the Liaodong Wall of the Ming, it was meant to prevent Han Chinese migration into Manchuria.<ref>Elliott, Mark C. "The Limits of Tartary: Manchuria in Imperial and National Geographies". ''Journal of Asian Studies'' 59, no. 3 (2000): 603–646.</ref>

===Foreign accounts===
[[File:Part of the Great Wall of China (April 1853, X, p.41) - Copy.jpg|thumb|Part of the Great Wall of China (April 1853, X, p. 41)<ref name="Juvenile1853">{{cite journal |title=Part of the Great Wall of China |journal=The Wesleyan Juvenile Offering: A Miscellany of Missionary Information for Young Persons |date=April 1853 |volume=X |page=[https://archive.org/details/wesleyanjuvenil19socigoog/page/n51 41] |url=https://archive.org/details/wesleyanjuvenil19socigoog |access-date=February 29, 2016 }}</ref>]]
[[Image:Greatwall large.jpg|thumb|The Great Wall in 1907]]
None of the [[Europeans in Medieval China|Europeans]] who visited China or Mongolia in the 13th and 14th centuries, such as [[Giovanni da Pian del Carpine]], [[William of Rubruck]], [[Marco Polo]], [[Odoric of Pordenone]] and [[Giovanni de' Marignolli]], mentioned the Great Wall.<ref>{{cite book |last=Ruysbroek |first=Willem van |url=https://archive.org/details/journeywilliamr01ruysgoog |title=The Journey of William of Rubruck to the Eastern Parts of the World, 1253–55, as Narrated by Himself, with Two Accounts of the Earlier Journey of John of Pian de Carpine |location=London |publisher=The Hakluyt Society |orig-year=1255 |year=1900 |others=Translated from the Latin by William Woodville Rockhill }}</ref>{{sfn|Haw|2006|pp=53–54}}

The North African traveler [[Ibn Battuta]], who also visited China during the [[Yuan dynasty]] {{Circa|1346}}, had heard about China's Great Wall, possibly before he had arrived in China.{{sfn|Haw|2006|pp=54–55}} He wrote that the wall is "sixty days' travel" from Zeitun (modern [[Quanzhou]]) in his travelogue ''[[Gift to Those Who Contemplate the Wonders of Cities and the Marvels of Travelling]]''. He associated it with the [[Gates of Alexander|legend of the wall]] mentioned in the [[Qur'an]],<ref>''[[Qur'an]]'', XVIII: "The Cave". English translations hosted at [[:s:Main Page|Wikisource]] include [[:s:The Holy Qur'an (Maulana Muhammad Ali)/18. The Cave|Maulana Muhammad Ali]]'s, [[:s:The Qur'an (Palmer)/Kahf|E.H. Palmer]]'s, and the [[:s:Quran (Progressive Muslims Organization)/18|Progressive Muslims Organization]]'s.</ref> which [[Dhul-Qarnayn]] (commonly associated with [[Alexander the Great]]) was said to have erected to protect people near the land of the rising sun from the savages of [[Gog and Magog]]. However, Ibn Battuta could find no one who had either seen it or knew of anyone who had seen it, suggesting that although there were remnants of the wall at that time, they were not significant.{{sfn|Haw|2006|pp=53–55}}<ref>{{cite book | editor1-last = Gibb | editor1-first = H.A.R.| editor1-link=Hamilton Alexander Rosskeen Gibb | editor2-last = Beckingham | editor2-first = C.F. |editor2-link=Charles Fraser Beckingham|year = 1994 | title = The Travels of Ibn Baṭṭūṭa, A.D. 1325–1354 (Volume 4) | place=London | publisher=Hakluyt Society | isbn = 978-0-904180-37-4 | pp=896-897 }}</ref>

Soon after the Portuguese reached Ming China by ship in the early 16th century, accounts of the Great Wall began circulating in Europe, although no European was able to see it for another 100 years. Possibly one of the earliest European descriptions of the wall and of its significance for the defense of the country against the "[[Tartars]]" (i.e. Mongols) may be the one contained in [[João de Barros]]'s 1563 ''Asia''.<ref>{{cite book |last=Barros |first=João de |title=Ásia de João de Barros: Dos feitos que os portugueses fizeram no descobrimento dos mares e terras do Oriente |at=''3a Década'', pp.&nbsp;186–204 (originally Vol. II, Ch. vii) |volume=V |location=Lisbon |orig-year=1563 |date=1777 |url=http://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=nyp.33433082328869;seq=232 |publisher=Lisboa |archive-date=November 2, 2018 |access-date=July 26, 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181102012705/http://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=nyp.33433082328869;seq=232 |url-status=live }}</ref> Other early accounts in Western sources include those of [[Gaspar da Cruz]], [[Bento de Goes]], [[Matteo Ricci]], and Bishop [[Juan González de Mendoza]],{{sfn|Waldron|1990|pp=204–05}} the latter in 1585 describing it as a "superbious and mightie work" of architecture, though he had not seen it.<ref name=Lach>{{cite book |last=Lach |first=Donald F |title=Asia in the Making of Europe |volume=I |publisher=The University of Chicago Press |date=1965 |page=769 }}</ref> In 1559, in his work "A Treatise of China and the Adjoyning Regions", Gaspar da Cruz offers an early discussion of the Great Wall.{{sfn|Waldron|1990|pp=204–05}} Perhaps the first recorded instance of a European actually entering China via the Great Wall came in 1605, when the Portuguese Jesuit brother [[Bento de Góis]] reached the northwestern [[Jiayu Pass]] from India.{{sfn|Yule|1866|p=579|ps=This section is the report of Góis's travel, as reported by [[Matteo Ricci]] in ''[[De Christiana expeditione apud Sinas]]'' (published 1615), annotated by [[Henry Yule]]).}} Early European accounts were mostly modest and empirical, closely mirroring contemporary Chinese understanding of the Wall,{{sfn|Waldron|1990|pp=2–4}} although later they slid into hyperbole,{{sfn|Waldron|1990|p=206}} including the erroneous but ubiquitous claim that the Ming walls were the same ones that were built by the first emperor in the 3rd century BC.{{sfn|Waldron|1990|p=206}}

When China opened its borders to foreign merchants and visitors after its defeat in the [[First Opium War|First]] and [[Second Opium War]]s, the Great Wall became a main attraction for tourists. The [[Travelogue (literature)|travelogues]] of the later 19th century further enhanced the reputation and the mythology of the Great Wall.{{sfn|Waldron|1990|p=209}}

{{anchor|Inner Great Wall}}<!--linked-->

==Course==
A formal definition of what constitutes a "Great Wall" has not been agreed upon, making the full course of the Great Wall difficult to describe in its entirety.{{sfn|Hessler|2007|p=60}} The defensive lines contain multiple stretches of ramparts, trenches and ditches, as well as individual fortresses.

In 2012, based on existing research and the results of a comprehensive mapping survey, the [[National Cultural Heritage Administration]] of China concluded that the remaining Great Wall associated sites include 10,051 wall sections, 1,764 ramparts or trenches, 29,510 individual buildings, and 2,211 fortifications or passes, with the walls and trenches spanning a total length of {{cvt|21196.18|km|mi}}.<ref name="ncha">{{cite web |script-title=zh:中国长城保护报告 |trans-title=Protection Report of the Great Wall of China |url=http://www.ncha.gov.cn/art/2016/11/30/art_722_135294.html |website=National Cultural Heritage Administration |access-date=March 3, 2021 |archive-date=December 19, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191219120648/http://www.ncha.gov.cn/art/2016/11/30/art_722_135294.html |url-status=live }}</ref> It was further concluded that the [[Ming Great Wall]] measures {{cvt|8850|km|mi}}.<ref name="Great Wall of China 'even longer'"/> This consists of {{cvt|6259|km|mi}} of wall sections, {{cvt|359|km|mi}} of trenches and {{cvt|2232|km|mi}} of natural defensive barriers such as hills and rivers.<ref name="Great Wall of China 'even longer'">{{cite news |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/asia-pacific/8008108.stm |title=Great Wall of China 'even longer' |publisher=BBC |date=April 20, 2009 |access-date=April 20, 2009 |archive-date=April 28, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090428021944/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/asia-pacific/8008108.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> In addition, [[Qin dynasty|Qin]], [[Han dynasty|Han]] and earlier Great Wall sites are {{cvt|3080|km|mi|0}} long in total; [[Jin dynasty (1115–1234)]] [[Jin dynasty (1115–1234)#Jin Great Wall|border fortifications]] are {{cvt|4010|km|mi|0}} in length; the remainder date back to [[Northern Wei]], [[Northern Qi]], [[Sui dynasty|Sui]], [[Tang dynasty|Tang]], the [[Five Dynasties]], [[Song dynasty|Song]], [[Liao dynasty|Liao]] and [[Xixia]].<ref name="ncha"/> About half of the sites are located in [[Inner Mongolia]] and [[Hebei]] (31% and 19% respectively).<ref name="ncha"/>

=== Han Great Wall ===
[[File:Hanmuren.JPG|thumb|Great Wall of Han dynasty near [[Yumenguan]]]]

Han fortifications start from [[Yumen Pass]] and [[Yang Pass]], southwest of [[Dunhuang]], in [[Gansu]] province. Ruins of the remotest Han border posts are found in Mamitu ({{nowrap|<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|馬|迷途}}}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|马|迷途}}}},}} ''Mǎmítú'', {{nowrap|<small>[[Literal translation|l]]</small> "horses losing their way"}}) near Yumen Pass.

=== Ming Great Wall ===
[[File:Jiayuguan Fortress 2008.jpg|thumb|[[Jiayu Pass|Jiayuguan]], the westmost point of Ming Great Wall]]
The [[Jiayuguan (pass)|Jiayu Pass]], located in Gansu province, is the western terminus of the Ming Great Wall. From here, the wall travels discontinuously down the [[Hexi Corridor]] and into the deserts of [[Ningxia]], where it enters the western edge of the Yellow River loop at [[Yinchuan]]. Here the first major walls erected during the Ming dynasty cut through the [[Ordos Desert]] to the eastern edge of the Yellow River loop. There, at Piantou Pass {{nowrap|(<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|偏|頭|關}}}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|偏|头|关}}}},}} ''Piāntóuguān'') in [[Xinzhou]], [[Shanxi]], the Great Wall splits in two with the "Outer Great Wall" {{nowrap|(<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|外|長城}}}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|外|长城}}}},}} ''Wài Chǎngchéng'') extending along the Inner Mongolia border with Shanxi into [[Hebei]] province, and the "Inner Great Wall" {{nowrap|(<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|內|長城}}}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|內|长城}}}},}} ''Nèi Chǎngchéng'') running southeast from Piantou Pass for some {{cvt|400|km}}, passing through important passes like the [[Pingxing Pass]] and [[Yanmen Pass]] before joining the Outer Great Wall at Sihaiye {{nowrap|({{lang|zh|{{linktext|四海|冶}}}}, ''Sìhǎiyě''),}} in Beijing's [[Yanqing County]].

The sections of the Great Wall around Beijing were frequently renovated, and are regularly visited by tourists today. The [[Badaling]] Great Wall near [[Zhangjiakou]] is the most famous stretch of the wall, as it was the first section to be opened to the public in the People's Republic of China; foreign dignitaries would be shown this section on visits to the Great Wall.{{sfn|Rojas|2010|p=140}} The Badaling Great Wall saw nearly 10 million visitors in 2018, and in 2019, a daily limit of 65,000 visitors was instated.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Askhar |first1=Aybek |title=Limit placed on number of visitors to Great Wall |url=https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/201905/29/WS5ceddf96a3104842260be639.html |website=China Daily |access-date=August 10, 2020 |archive-date=September 7, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200907070654/http://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/201905/29/WS5ceddf96a3104842260be639.html |url-status=live }}</ref> South of Badaling is the [[Juyong Pass]]; when it was used by the Chinese to protect their land, this section of the wall had many guards to defend the capital Beijing. Made of stone and bricks from the hills, this portion of the Great Wall is {{cvt|7.8|m|ftin}} high and {{cvt|5|m|ftin}} wide.

[[File:20090529 Great Wall 8185.jpg|thumb|Ming dynasty Great Wall at [[Jinshanling]]]]

One of the most striking sections of the Ming Great Wall is where it climbs extremely steep slopes in [[Jinshanling]]. There it runs {{cvt|11|km|mi|0}} long, ranges from {{cvt|5|to|8|m|ftin}} in height, and {{cvt|6|m|ftin}} across the bottom, narrowing up to {{cvt|5|m|ftin}} across the top. Wangjing Lou {{nowrap|(<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|望|京|樓}}}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|望|京|楼}}}},}} ''Wàngjīng Lóu'') is one of Jinshanling's 67 [[watchtowers]], {{cvt|980|m|ft}} above sea level. Southeast of Jinshanling is the [[Mutianyu]] Great Wall which winds along lofty, cragged mountains from the southeast to the northwest for {{cvt|2.25|km|mi}}. It is connected with Juyongguan Pass to the west and Gubeikou to the east. This section was one of the first to be renovated following the turmoil of the [[Cultural Revolution]].{{sfn|Lindesay|2008|p=212}}
[[File:Grande Muraille de Chine Mutianyu en 2015.jpg|thumb|Great Wall at Mutianyu 2015]]

At the edge of the Bohai Gulf is [[Shanhai Pass]], considered the traditional end of the Great Wall and the "First Pass [[Tianxia|Under Heaven]]". The part of the wall inside Shanhai Pass that meets the sea is named the "Old Dragon Head". {{cvt|3|km|mi|0}} north of Shanhai Pass is Jiaoshan Great Wall ({{nowrap|<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{linktext|焦山|長城}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{linktext|焦|山|长城}},}} ''Jiāoshān Chángchéng''), the site of the first mountain of the Great Wall.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.travelchinaguide.com/china_great_wall/scene/hebei/jiaoshan.htm |title=Jiaoshan Great Wall |access-date=September 15, 2010 |work=TravelChinaGuide.com |quote=Jiaoshan Great Wall is located about {{cvt|3|km|mi|0 }} from Shanhaiguan ancient city. It is named after Jiaoshan Mountain, which is the highest peak to the north of Shanhai Pass and also the first mountain the Great Wall climbs up after Shanhai Pass. Therefore Jiaoshan Mountain is noted as "The first mountain of the Great Wall". |archive-date=January 19, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100119050932/http://www.travelchinaguide.com/china_great_wall/scene/hebei/jiaoshan.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> {{cvt|15|km|0}} northeast from Shanhaiguan is [[:zh:九门口|Jiumenkou]] {{nowrap|(<small>[[traditional characters|t]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|九|門|口}}}},}} {{nowrap|<small>[[simplified characters|s]]</small> {{lang|zh|{{linktext|九|门|口}}}},}} ''Jiǔménkǒu''), which is the only portion of the wall that was built as a bridge.

In 2009, 180&nbsp;km of previously unknown sections of the Ming wall concealed by hills, trenches and rivers were discovered with the help of infrared range finders and [[GPS]] devices.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2009/apr/20/great-wall-china-discovery |title=Great Wall of China longer than believed as 180 missing miles found |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |date=April 20, 2009 |access-date=April 18, 2015 |agency=Associated Press |archive-date=April 18, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150418133312/http://www.theguardian.com/world/2009/apr/20/great-wall-china-discovery |url-status=live }}</ref> In March and April 2015, nine sections with a total length of more than {{cvt|10|km|mi|0}}, believed to be part of the Great Wall, were discovered along the border of [[Ningxia]] autonomous region and Gansu province.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://usa.chinadaily.com.cn/culture/2015-04/15/content_20441982.htm |title=Newly-discovered remains redraw path of Great Wall |work=[[China Daily]] |date=April 15, 2015 |access-date=April 18, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150418134751/http://usa.chinadaily.com.cn/culture/2015-04/15/content_20441982.htm |archive-date=April 18, 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Characteristics==
[[File:Henry William Parish - Plan, Section and Geometrical Elevation of the Great Wall and a Tower, near the Pass at Cou-pe-keou.jpg|thumb|right|1793 technical drawings of the Great Wall produced during the [[Macartney Embassy]] to Qing dynasty.]] 
Before the use of bricks, the Great Wall was mainly built from rammed earth, stones, and wood. During the Ming, however, bricks were heavily used in many areas of the wall, as were materials such as tiles, [[lime (mineral)|lime]], and stone. The size and weight of the bricks made them easier to work with than earth and stone, so construction quickened. Additionally, bricks could bear more weight and endure better than rammed earth. Stone can hold under its own weight better than brick, but is more difficult to use. Consequently, stones cut into rectangular shapes were used for the foundation, inner and outer [[:wikt:brim|brims]], and gateways of the wall. [[Battlement]]s line the uppermost portion of the vast majority of the wall, with defensive gaps a little over {{cvt|30|cm}} tall, and about {{cvt|23|cm}} wide. From the parapets, guards could survey the surrounding land.{{sfn|Turnbull|2007|p=29}} 

[[Sticky rice mortar]], consisting of sticky rice soup mixed with [[Calcium hydroxide|slaked lime]], was extensively used to hold bricks together;<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=July 6, 2010 |title=Sticky rice porridge and the Great Wall of China |url=https://www.world-archaeology.com/world/asia/china/sticky-rice-porridge-and-the-great-wall-of-china/ |access-date=July 6, 2022 |website=[[World Archaeology]] |language=en-US }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Boissoneault |first=Lorraine |date=February 16, 2017 |title=Sticky Rice Mortar, the View From Space, and More Fun Facts About China's Great Wall |url=https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/sticky-rice-mortar-view-space-and-more-fun-facts-about-chinas-great-wall-180962197/ |access-date=July 6, 2022 |website=[[Smithsonian (magazine)|Smithsonian]] |language=en |archive-date=December 1, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211201222611/https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/sticky-rice-mortar-view-space-and-more-fun-facts-about-chinas-great-wall-180962197/ |url-status=live }}</ref> no human [[Bone|bones]] or body parts were ever incorporated into the mortar or any part of the wall, contrary to urban legend.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nanos |first=Janelle |date=November 12, 2010 |title=Slide Down the Great Wall of China |url=https://www.nationalgeographic.com/travel/article/slide_down_the_great_wall |access-date=July 6, 2022 |website=[[National Geographic]] |language=en |quote=[...] (in fact, there have been no bones, human or otherwise, found in the Wall, though a great number of workers did die while toiling to build it). }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Horsford |first=Simon |date=February 17, 2017 |title=Five myths about the Great Wall of China |language=en-GB |work=[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]] |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/travel/destinations/asia/china/articles/myths-about-the-great-wall-of-china/ |url-access=subscription |access-date=July 6, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170220111205/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/travel/destinations/asia/china/articles/myths-about-the-great-wall-of-china/ |archive-date=February 20, 2017 |issn=0307-1235 |quote=No bones or indeed other indication of human remains have been found in the Wall. }}</ref> Communication between the army units along the length of the Great Wall, including the ability to call reinforcements and warn [[garrisons]] of enemy movements, was of high importance. Signal towers were built upon hill tops or other high points along the wall for their visibility. Wooden gates could be used as a trap against those going through. Barracks, stables, and armories were built near the wall's inner surface.{{sfn|Turnbull|2007|p=29}}

==Condition==
[[Image:A long stretch of the Great Wall.jpg|thumb|right|A more rural portion of the Great Wall that stretches through the mountains, here seen in slight disrepair]]

While portions north of [[Beijing]] and near tourist centers have been preserved and even extensively renovated, in many other locations the wall is in disrepair. The wall sometimes provided a source of stones to build houses and roads.<ref>Ford, Peter (November 30, 2006). [https://www.csmonitor.com/2006/1130/p07s02-woap.html New law to keep China's Wall looking great]. Christian Science Monitor, Asia Pacific section. Retrieved March 17, 2007.</ref> Sections of the wall are also prone to [[graffiti]] and [[vandalism]], while inscribed bricks were pilfered and sold on the market for up to 50 [[renminbi]].<ref name="30percent" /> Parts have been destroyed to make way for construction or mining.<ref>[http://www.chinaheritagequarterly.org/features.php?searchterm=001_greatwall.inc&issue=001 Bruce G. Doar: ''The Great Wall of China: Tangible, Intangible and Destructible.''] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120815120208/http://www.chinaheritagequarterly.org/features.php?searchterm=001_greatwall.inc&issue=001 |date=August 15, 2012 }} China Heritage Newsletter, China Heritage Project, [[Australian National University]]</ref> 

A 2012 report by the [[National Cultural Heritage Administration]] states that 22% of the Ming Great Wall, or {{cvt|1961|km|mi}}, has disappeared.<ref name="30percent">{{cite web |url=http://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/29/disappearance-of-chinas-great-wall-prompts-concerns/ |title=China Fears Loss of Great Wall, Brick by Brick |last=Wong |first=Edward |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=June 29, 2015 |access-date=July 1, 2015 |archive-date=July 1, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150701124253/http://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/06/29/disappearance-of-chinas-great-wall-prompts-concerns/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2007 it was estimated that more than {{cvt|60|km|mi}} of the wall in [[Gansu]] province may disappear in the next 20 years, due to erosion from [[Dust storm|sandstorms]]. In some places, the height of the wall has been reduced from more than {{cvt|5|m|ftin}} to less than {{cvt|2|m|ftin}}. Various square lookout towers that characterize the most famous images of the wall have disappeared. Many western sections of the wall are [[Mudbrick|constructed from mud]], rather than brick and stone, and thus are more susceptible to erosion.<ref>{{cite news |title=China's Wall becoming less and less Great |work=Reuters |date=August 29, 2007 |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/latestCrisis/idUSPEK274699 |access-date=August 30, 2007 |archive-date=December 21, 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071221121333/http://www.reuters.com/article/latestCrisis/idUSPEK274699 |url-status=live }}</ref> In 2014 a portion of the wall near the border of Liaoning and Hebei province was repaired with concrete. The work has been much criticized.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cnn.com/2016/09/21/asia/great-wall-china-cement-repair/index.html |title=China's Great Wall covered in cement |author1=Ben Westcott |author2=Serenitie Wang |website=[[CNN]] |date=September 21, 2016 |access-date=September 22, 2016 |archive-date=January 22, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170122212325/http://www.cnn.com/2016/09/21/asia/great-wall-china-cement-repair/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

A section of the wall in Shanxi province was severely damaged in 2023 by construction workers, who widened an existing gap in the wall to make a shortcut for an excavator to pass through. Police described the act as causing "irreversible damage to the integrity of the Ming Great Wall and to the safety of the cultural relics".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-china-66714547 |title=China's Great Wall damaged by workers looking for shortcut |date=September 5, 2023 |via=www.bbc.co.uk |archive-date=September 28, 2023 |access-date=September 29, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230928183620/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-china-66714547 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Visibility from space==
Various [[Factoid|factoids]] in popular culture claim that the Great Wall can be seen (with the naked eye) from space, with questionable degrees of veracity. A significant barrier to that is that although the Wall is long enough, it is too narrow, and astronauts report that, as it is made of local materials, there is a camouflage effect as well.

===From the Moon===
The Great Wall of China cannot be seen by the naked human eye from the [[Moon]] which orbits around Earth at an average distance of 384,399 kilometres (238,854 mi).<ref>{{Cite web |title=NASA - China's Wall Less Great in View from Space |url=https://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html |access-date=June 7, 2021 |website=www.nasa.gov |language=en |archive-date=April 8, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210408001053/https://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Even though the myth has been thoroughly debunked, it is still ingrained in popular culture.<ref name="Debunked">[http://urbanlegends.about.com/b/2003/10/08/whats-visible-from-outer-space.htm Urban Legends.com website] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120303112932/http://urbanlegends.about.com/b/2003/10/08/whats-visible-from-outer-space.htm |date=March 3, 2012 }}. Accessed May 12, 2010.<br />"[http://geography.about.com/library/faq/blqzgreatwall.htm Can you see the Great Wall of China from the moon or outer space?] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130511044245/http://geography.about.com/library/faq/blqzgreatwall.htm |date=May 11, 2013 }}", Answers.com. Accessed May 12, 2010.<br />[[Cecil Adams]], "[http://www.straightdope.com/columns/read/417/is-the-great-wall-of-china-the-only-manmade-object-you-can-see-from-space Is the Great wall of China the only manmade object byou can see from space?] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120224175602/http://www.straightdope.com/columns/read/417/is-the-great-wall-of-china-the-only-manmade-object-you-can-see-from-space |date=February 24, 2012 }}", ''The Straight Dope''. Accessed May 12, 2010.<br />Snopes, "[http://www.snopes.com/science/greatwall.asp Great wall from space]", last updated July 21, 2007. Accessed May 12, 2010.<br />"[http://www.scientificamerican.com/article.cfm?id=is-chinas-great-wall-visible-from-space Is China's Great Wall Visible from Space?] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131217015535/http://www.scientificamerican.com/article.cfm?id=is-chinas-great-wall-visible-from-space |date=December 17, 2013 }}", ''Scientific American'', February 21, 2008. "...&nbsp;the wall is only visible from low orbit under a specific set of weather and lighting conditions. And many other structures that are less spectacular from an earthly vantage point—desert roads, for example—appear more prominent from an orbital perspective."</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Metro Tescos |url=https://www.thetimes.com/article/metro-tescos-j850gbmtj69 |work=[[The Times]] |date=April 26, 2010 |language=en |archive-date=January 15, 2025 |access-date=January 15, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250115104938/https://www.thetimes.com/article/metro-tescos-j850gbmtj69 |url-status=live }}</ref> The apparent width of the Great Wall as seen from the Moon would be the same as that of a human hair viewed from {{cvt|3|km|0}} away.{{sfn|López-Gil|2008|pp=3–4}}

One of the earliest known references to the myth of the Great Wall's visibility from the Moon appears in a letter written in 1754 by the English antiquary [[William Stukeley]]. Stukeley wrote that, "This mighty wall {{bracket|[[Hadrian's Wall]]}} of four score miles [130 km] in length is only exceeded by the Chinese Wall, which makes a considerable figure upon the [[terrestrial globe]], and may be discerned at the Moon."<ref>''The Family Memoirs of the Rev. William Stukeley'' (1887) Vol. 3, p. 142. (1754).</ref> The claim was also mentioned in 1895 by [[Sir Henry Norman, 1st Baronet|Henry Norman]], who wrote "besides its age it enjoys the reputation of being the only work of human hands on the globe visible from the Moon."<ref>Norman, Henry, ''The Peoples and Politics of the Far East'', p. 215. (1895).</ref> The myth also appears in the 1932 strip of ''[[Ripley's Believe It or Not!]].''<ref name="Ripley">"[https://web.archive.org/web/20110408182924/https://www.newscientist.com/blog/space/uploaded_images/great-wall-ripleys-775778.jpg "The Great Wall of China"], ''[[Ripley's Believe It or Not!]]'', 1932.</ref>

===From low Earth orbit===
[[File:Great Wall of China, Satellite image red composite.jpg|thumb|upright=1.5|Identical satellite images of a section of the Great Wall in northern Shanxi, running diagonally from lower left to upper right and not to be confused with the more prominent river running from upper left to lower right. In the image on the right, the Great Wall has been outlined in red. The region pictured is {{cvt|12|x|12|km|0}}.]]

The Great Wall has also been claimed to be visible from [[low Earth orbit]] (an altitude extending from {{cvt|100|mi|km|-1|order=flip}}). [[NASA]] states that it is barely visible, and only under nearly perfect conditions; it is no more conspicuous than many other human-made objects.<ref name=nasa>{{cite web |url=http://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html |title=NASA – Great Wall of China |publisher=Nasa.gov |access-date=July 31, 2010 |archive-date=May 8, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130508015306/http://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Astronauts testifying to its visibility from space include [[Eugene Cernan|Gene Cernan]]{{Efn|"At Earth orbit of 100 to 200 miles [160 to 320 km] high, the Great Wall of China is, indeed, visible to the naked eye."}} and [[Ed Lu]];{{Efn|"It's less visible than a lot of other objects. And you have to know where to look."}} [[Yang Liwei]], China's first astronaut, meanwhile stated that he had not been able to see it. In response, the [[European Space Agency]] (ESA) issued a press release reporting of its visibility from an altitude of {{cvt|160|and|320|km|mi|-1}};{{sfn|López-Gil|2008|pp=3–4}} the image was actually of a river in Beijing.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://en.people.cn/200405/21/eng20040521_144014.html |title=People's Daily Online -- ESA admits "Great Wal" on satellite photo a mistake |access-date=September 4, 2022 |archive-date=September 4, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220904155312/http://en.people.cn/200405/21/eng20040521_144014.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[Leroy Chiao]], a Chinese-American astronaut, took a photograph from the [[International Space Station]] that shows the wall. It was so indistinct that the photographer was not certain he had actually captured it. Based on the photograph, the ''[[China Daily]]'' later reported that the Great Wall can be seen from 'space' with the naked eye, under favorable viewing conditions, if one knows exactly where to look.<ref name=Francis>Markus, Francis. (April 19, 2005). [http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/asia-pacific/4459311.stm Great Wall visible in space photo] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130412094748/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/asia-pacific/4459311.stm |date=April 12, 2013 }}. BBC News, Asia-Pacific section. Retrieved March 17, 2007.</ref>{{sfn|López-Gil|2008|pp=3–4}}

